  "commentary_text": "Another problem with MOST switches is that the terminals are connected by parasitic capacitances. In a MOST they are the overlap capacitances. The larger the widths (for smaller R ), on the larger the overlap capacitors. The clock pulses are partially injected in the signal path. Indeed, the overlap capacitor C forms a ovl capacitive divider with the storage capacitor C. if C is about 1 fF and ovl\n\nC is 1 pF, then about 0.1% of the clock pulse is injected in the signal path. For a clock of 3 V, this is a 3 mV error signal in the signal path. This gives a contribution at the clock frequency and its harmonics! It does not affect the lower frequency bands, however. The charge itself, transferred by C , is of the order of fC. It is quite small but not negligible. ovl",
